ping in the  last really the last half century but  particularly the last 10 years and one  move that I noticed was from an idea of  comparative literature as  multiculturalism  where each culture as I was saying just  a moment ago right this model of each  culture being sort of like a planet that  spins on its own axis to attention to  things that cover a lot of different  cultures that you might call  globalization right so multiculturalism  would tend to make us isolate cultures  and think of cultures as having their  own constituencies and their own ways of  being and a force like globalization  think of it as economic globalization or  the the mobility of Labor for example  today that we see in the economic world  these are these are like tsunamis  they're like big waves that cover  everything in their path and there are  such things if you're only interested in  the multicultural angle of difference  you might be oblivious to these big  dynamics and yet how does the big  universal dynamic intersect with the way  that we comparative literature people  focus on difference in specificity that  was what was interesting to me and how  does it well we we exhibit this relation  I think in some of the things that we  pay attention to fifteen years ago  people were not paying attention to  things like world literature which has  since grown up to be a big obsession of  many people in the field and what it  what is world literature it's the the  traffic among nations that causes some  authors to become prominent on a global  scale and of courses have been going on  for a long time almost 200 years ago  Goethe named world literate Ori said  that the age of world literature is  coming and he could be a good example of  it because by the time he died everybody  on the planet who read works of  literature knew about Goethe right other  writers might not have had such a  promising beginning but they grow to  have worldwide significance you know  people like Baudelaire let's 